But R1: "NEVER change production code without a test that first reproduces it" — the reproduction here is the CI gate itself (crap_report.json artifact, maxCrap 90 named to this method). I'll reproduce it locally before the edit (download shards, merge, run crap4dart → RED 90), then GREEN after.

Then the guard scanner suggestion (thread 4):
4. `maskedForScan`: handle string interpolation `${...}` — inside a string, on `${`, enter code mode until the matching `}` (nested braces counted). Within that code span, nested strings/comments... for scanner purposes: keep it simple — after `${`, consume until the matching `}` counting braces, keeping INTERIOR as code? The reviewer says "Exact-parens matching of ${...} nesting would remove the imprecision". Simple approach: when inside a single/double-quoted string and we see `$` followed by `{`, we treat the interpolation as CODE (keep chars verbatim? or mask?) — the issue: `'${a['k']}'` — the masker currently closes at the inner `'` leaving `k']} ...` as code → junk code tokens (`]}`, etc.) — junk CODE never contains `JsonWidgetRenderer(`, but junk parens CAN desync balancedArgs. If I treat `${...}` as part of the string interior (mask it, count nesting), the whole interpolation is masked → no desync. Interpolation CODE containing `JsonWidgetRenderer(...)`? Contrived beyond reason (a widget tree built inside a string interpolation). Masking the whole `${...}` span is safe: masking never ADDS matches. And it fixes the desync. Implementation: in consumeString, on `$` + `{`, scan forward counting brace depth until matching `}` (masking all of it), continue the string. `${}` braces inside nested strings-of-interpolation — recursively nested strings inside interpolation (`'${a["s"]}'`) — the interior could contain another string with braces... full recursion is overkill; count braces while masking; a nested string containing `{`/`}` could desync the count — FALSE-RED direction only (documented). Keep it simple + document.

5. Per-site args: strip nested balanced `(...)` groups before `contains('voxelWorld:')`:
```dart
String topLevelArgs(String args) {
  final out = StringBuffer();
  var depth = 0;
  for (var i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
    final ch = args[i];
    if (ch == '(') depth++;
    if (depth == 0) out.write(ch); 
    if (ch == ') depth--;
  }
  return out.toString();
}
```
Hmm — depth==0 write: writes chars at top level; when entering a nested group, the `(` itself: depth++ BEFORE the write check → the `(` is not written (good — drop nested groups entirely). Closing `)`: written? ch==')' → depth-- first → depth becomes 0 → then `if (depth==0) write` — wait order: I do `if (ch=='(') depth++;` then `if (depth == 0) out.write(ch);` then `if (ch==') depth--;` — for `)`: at check time depth is still ≥1 (inner) → not written; then depth--. Good: writes only top-level chars incl. the outer `(`/`)` (for the outer `(`: depth++ makes 1 → not written; hmm the outer args string starts WITH `(`. Losing the parens is fine — only `contains('voxelWorld:')` matters). Edge: a `-` before a number... irrelevant. This drops every nested (...) span. But it must run on MASKED text (string interiors already spaces) — else parens in strings would desync. The guard calls it on the captured masked args. 

Then the guard's main assertion becomes `expect(topLevelArgs(args), contains('voxelWorld:'))` — and reason text updated. The knownSurfaces floor + everything else unchanged.

New pins:
- nested-call shape: `final r = JsonWidgetRenderer(theme: resolve(voxelWorld: w), onEvent: _);` → raw captured args contain voxelWorld: BUT topLevelArgs does NOT → guard would RED (correct per reviewer: the construction omits the param). Pin: `expect(topLevelArgs(args), isNot(contains('voxelWorld:')))` + `expect(args, contains('voxelWorld:'))`.
- direct-arg shape still green: `JsonWidgetRenderer(voxelWorld: w, theme: resolve(x))` → topLevelArgs contains voxelWorld: (the nested group `(x)` stripped, top-level voxelWorld kept).
- interpolation desync: `final label = 'v: ${a['k']} (x)';` then a construction on the NEXT line with voxelWorld → previously the `']}` junk could... construct the desync: `final s = 'it's ${m['k']} )';` — hmm single-quote-in-single-quote interpolation. With the fix, whole span masked → balancedArgs fine. Pin: source with `final note = 'n: ${cfg['k']} )';` followed by `final r = JsonWidgetRenderer(onEvent: _, voxelWorld: w);` → construction found with args intact containing voxelWorld:.

6. Re-run root guard (RED first for new pins!), then GREEN after scanner changes. Order: write pins first, run (2 fail), implement, run (green).

